Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Pod Fails to Start with Vault Annotations
Symptoms: Pod remains in Init:0/1 or Init:Error state

Solutions:

# Check init container logs
kubectl logs $POD_NAME -c vault-agent-init

# Verify service account has correct permissions
kubectl describe serviceaccount myapp-sa

# Check if Vault role exists
kubectl exec -n vault vault-0 -- vault read auth/kubernetes/role/myapp
Issue 2: Secrets Not Appearing in Pod
Symptoms: /vault/secrets/ directory is empty

Solutions:

# Verify Vault policy allows reading secrets
kubectl exec -n vault vault-0 -- vault policy read myapp-policy

# Check if secrets exist in Vault
kubectl exec -n vault vault-0 -- vault kv get secret/myapp/config

# Verify annotation syntax in deployment
kubectl get pod $POD_NAME -o yaml | grep -A 20 annotations
Issue 3: Authentication Failures
Symptoms: Vault agent cannot authenticate

Solutions:

# Check Kubernetes auth configuration
kubectl exec -n vault vault-0 -- vault read auth/kubernetes/config

# Verify service account token
kubectl get secret vault-auth-secret -n vault -o yaml

# Test manual authentication
kubectl exec -n vault vault-0 -- vault write auth/kubernetes/login role=myapp jwt="$(kubectl get secret vault-auth-secret -n vault -o jsonpath='{.data.token}' | base64 --decode)"
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

Set up a complete Kubernetes environment with kind, including Docker, kubectl, and Helm
Installed HashiCorp Vault on Kubernetes using Helm charts with proper configuration
Configured Kubernetes authentication for Vault, enabling secure communication between Kubernetes and Vault
Implemented secret injection using Vault Agent Injector to automatically provide secrets to pods
Created and deployed applications that consume secrets from Vault in various formats
Tested secret updates and rotation to understand how applications receive updated credentials
Learned troubleshooting techniques for common Vault-Kubernetes integration issues
This integration provides several important benefits:

Enhanced Security: Secrets are never stored in Kubernetes etcd or container images
Dynamic Secret Management: Secrets can be rotated and updated without rebuilding applications
Centralized Secret Storage: All secrets are managed in one secure location
Audit Trail: Vault provides comprehensive logging of secret access
Fine-grained Access Control: Policies ensure applications only access necessary secrets
The skills you've learned in this lab are essential for implementing secure secret management in production Kubernetes environments. Vault's integration with Kubernetes provides a robust foundation for managing sensitive data in containerized applications while maintaining security best practices.

Understanding this integration is crucial for DevOps engineers and security professionals working with modern containerized applications, as it addresses one of the most critical aspects of application security: protecting sensitive configuration data and credentials.
